window.SITE.handouts.push(
 /* ================= WEEK 5 · PART 1 ================= */
 {n:11, wk:5, title:"Perseus and the Heroes of Argos", sub:"The pattern of the hero, Medusa, and Andromeda",
  overview:{img:"h11_cellini", cap:["Benvenuto Cellini","Perseus with the Head of Medusa","1545–54"],
   sum:"Greek heroes are mortal sons of gods whose lives follow a familiar pattern: a strange birth, a dangerous quest, help from the gods, monsters defeated, and a reward at the end. Perseus, the great hero of Argos, fits the pattern almost perfectly. His family tree also links many myths of the Argive plain, from Io and the Danaïds to his great-grandson Heracles.",
   bul:["Heroes follow a shared pattern of folktale motifs","Perseus: hero of Argos and ancestor of Heracles","Argive line: Io → Danaïds → Danaë → Perseus"],
   terms:[["Folktale motifs in heroic myth","Divine parent, miraculous birth, great strength, a loyal companion, impossible tasks set by an enemy, a broken taboo, a dangerous woman resisted, a quest (often to the underworld), help from gods, return and atonement, a great reward, and a hero’s funeral or deification."],["Inachus and Melia","The river god of Argos and an ash-tree nymph, parents of Io in some versions."],["Epaphus","Son of Zeus and Io, born in Egypt; ancestor of Danaus, Acrisius, and Perseus."],["Acrisius","King of Argos who locked his daughter Danaë away because an oracle said her son would kill him."]]},
  slides:[
  {type:"theme", title:"The pattern of the hero", img:"h11_rubens", stmt:"Heroic myths are built from the same parts, rearranged.",
   para:"Perseus has a god for a father and a miraculous birth, is sent on an impossible quest by a hostile king, receives magical gifts from the gods, kills a monster, rescues a princess, and returns to punish his enemy. Heracles, Theseus, and Jason follow versions of the same plan. Recognizing the pattern helps explain why these stories feel familiar and why they were easy to retell.",
   bul:["Divine birth, quest, monster, reward","Help from gods and magical objects","Same plan behind Heracles, Theseus, Jason"],
   terms:[], works:["h11_graiae","h11_cellini","h11_rubens"]},
  {type:"work", key:"h11_graiae", artist:"Edward Burne-Jones", title:"Perseus and the Graiae", date:"ca. 1875–78", det:"Gesso relief with gold and silver leaf on oak panel · National Museum Cardiff",
   fn:"One panel of a Perseus cycle commissioned by the politician Arthur Balfour for the music room of his London house.",
   sum:"Danaë and the infant Perseus were set adrift in a chest and washed ashore on the island of Seriphos, where the fisherman Dictys took them in. King Polydectes, wanting Danaë, sent Perseus to fetch the head of the Gorgon Medusa. To find her, Perseus seized the single eye and tooth shared by the Graiae, the Gorgons’ gray-haired sisters, until they gave him directions. Burne-Jones shows him snatching the eye as the three blind sisters grope for it.",
   bul:["Danaë and Perseus washed ashore on Seriphos","Polydectes sends Perseus for Medusa’s head","Perseus steals the Graiae’s shared eye"],
   terms:[["Dictys and Polydectes","Brothers on Seriphos: the kind fisherman who rescued Danaë, and the king who sent Perseus away so he could force her into marriage."],["Graiae","“The gray ones,” three old sisters of the Gorgons who share one eye and one tooth."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h11_selinunte", artist:"", title:"Metopes from Temple C at Selinus", date:"Greek (Sicily), ca. 540–530 BCE", det:"Limestone · Regional Archaeological Museum, Palermo",
   fn:"Carved panels from the frieze of a temple, probably to Apollo, in the Greek colony of Selinus in Sicily.",
   sum:"The center metope shows Perseus cutting off Medusa’s head while Athena stands by and Medusa clutches the winged horse Pegasus. The Gorgons were three sisters: Stheno (“strength”), Euryale (“far-leaper”), and the mortal Medusa (“ruler”). Perseus used the gifts of the gods, winged sandals, a cap of invisibility, a curved sword, and a bag, and looked only at her reflection to avoid being turned to stone. From her severed neck sprang Pegasus and Chrysaor, her sons by Poseidon.",
   bul:["Perseus beheads Medusa with Athena’s help","Gifts: sandals, cap, sword, bag","Pegasus and Chrysaor spring from her neck"],
   terms:[["Gorgons","Stheno, Euryale, and Medusa, snake-haired sisters whose gaze turns people to stone; only Medusa was mortal."],["Metope","A square panel, often carved, set between the grooved blocks of a Doric temple frieze."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h11_caravaggio", artist:"Caravaggio", title:"Medusa", date:"ca. 1597–98", det:"Oil on canvas mounted on a wooden shield · Uffizi Gallery, Florence",
   fn:"A ceremonial parade shield, given by Cardinal Francesco del Monte to Ferdinando I de’ Medici, Grand Duke of Tuscany.",
   sum:"Even after death, Medusa’s head kept its power, and Perseus gave it to Athena, who set it on her aegis. Images of the Gorgon’s face were placed on shields, armor, and temples to frighten away enemies and evil. Caravaggio paints the head at the instant of beheading, still screaming, on a real convex shield, so the viewer, like Perseus, sees her only as a reflection.",
   bul:["Medusa’s head kept its petrifying power","Gorgon face as a protective device","Painted on a real shield"],
   terms:[["Gorgoneion","An image of the Gorgon’s face used on shields, armor, and buildings to ward off evil."]]},
  {type:"theme", title:"The face that protects", img:"h11_baleful", stmt:"The monster’s deadly face becomes a weapon against danger.",
   para:"Medusa’s gaze kills, but once she is defeated her face turns into protection. Greeks painted Gorgon faces on shields and roof tiles, and Athena wears one on her breastplate. Later artists from Cellini to Caravaggio used the severed head as a symbol of power over enemies, and modern writers have read her as an image of fear of female power.",
   bul:["Deadly gaze turned into protection","On shields, armor, temples, and Athena’s aegis","A symbol of power over enemies"],
   terms:[["Apotropaic","Meant to turn away evil or bad luck, like a Gorgon face on a shield."]], works:["h11_selinunte","h11_caravaggio","h11_cellini"]},
  {type:"work", key:"h11_cellini", artist:"Benvenuto Cellini", title:"Perseus with the Head of Medusa", date:"1545–54", det:"Bronze · Loggia dei Lanzi, Florence",
   fn:"Commissioned by Duke Cosimo I de’ Medici for the public square in front of the city hall, as a message about his power.",
   sum:"Perseus stands on Medusa’s body holding up her head, blood pouring from her neck in curling bronze streams. Cellini, a goldsmith, cast the statue in one daring pour that he described in his autobiography. Set near Donatello’s Judith and Michelangelo’s David, both symbols of the Florentine republic, the Perseus told citizens that Medici rule had beheaded its enemies.",
   bul:["Perseus holds up Medusa’s head","Cast in a single difficult pour","Political message for Medici Florence"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h11_titian", artist:"Titian", title:"Perseus and Andromeda", date:"1554–56", det:"Oil on canvas · Wallace Collection, London",
   fn:"One of the poesie, mythological paintings Titian made for King Philip II of Spain.",
   sum:"On his way home, Perseus found the Ethiopian princess Andromeda chained to a rock by the sea. Her mother, Queen Cassiopeia, had boasted that she was more beautiful than the Nereids, the sea nymphs, and Poseidon sent a sea monster to punish the kingdom; an oracle said only Andromeda’s sacrifice would end it. Titian shows Perseus diving down from the sky while the pale, chained princess twists away from the monster below.",
   bul:["Cassiopeia’s boast offends the Nereids","Andromeda chained as a sacrifice","Perseus swoops down to save her"],
   terms:[["Cepheus and Cassiopeia","King and queen of Ethiopia, parents of Andromeda; all three became constellations."],["Nereids","Sea nymphs, the fifty daughters of Nereus, including Amphitrite and Thetis."],["Ceto","A sea monster (or the sea goddess who mothered monsters) sent to attack Ethiopia."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h11_rubens", artist:"Peter Paul Rubens", title:"Perseus Freeing Andromeda", date:"ca. 1620–22", det:"Oil on canvas · Hermitage Museum, St. Petersburg",
   fn:"Rubens probably kept it in his own house in Antwerp until his death; it reached the Hermitage in 1769.",
   sum:"Rubens skips the fight and shows the aftermath: Perseus, crowned by Victory, gently frees Andromeda while cupids lead Pegasus and the dead monster lies below. Perseus later used Medusa’s head to turn Polydectes and his court to stone, returned the gifts of the gods, and, as foretold, accidentally killed his grandfather Acrisius with a discus. He then ruled Tiryns, and his granddaughter Alcmena’s son was Heracles.",
   bul:["Perseus frees Andromeda, crowned by Victory","Petrifies Polydectes; kills Acrisius by accident","Founds the royal line of Heracles"],
   terms:[["Andromeda","Ethiopian princess rescued by Perseus and later his wife; a constellation."]]},
  {type:"theme", title:"The girl’s tragedy", img:"h11_titian", stmt:"Many myths follow a young woman through loss, suffering, and rescue.",
   para:"Io, Danaë, and Andromeda all follow a pattern scholars call the girl’s tragedy: a princess is separated from her home, loved or threatened by a god or monster, punished or exposed, and finally rescued, often bearing a hero son. The story mirrors a Greek girl’s life, leaving her father’s house for marriage. In art it gave painters a reason to show female suffering and rescue.",
   bul:["Leaving home, threat, punishment, rescue","Mirrors the move from girl to bride","Io, Danaë, Andromeda"],
   terms:[], works:["h11_titian","h11_rubens","h11_baleful"]},
  {type:"work", key:"h11_baleful", artist:"Edward Burne-Jones", title:"The Baleful Head", date:"1885", det:"Gouache · Southampton City Art Gallery",
   fn:"One of the gouache designs for Burne-Jones’s Perseus cycle, commissioned by Arthur Balfour for the music room of his London house; this one plans the cycle’s last scene.",
   sum:"The cycle ends with Perseus showing Andromeda Medusa’s head reflected in the still water of a well, so she can see it safely. Burne-Jones turns the story’s violence into a hushed, dreamy love scene among apple trees. The mirror motif ties the ending back to the shield Perseus used to approach Medusa.",
   bul:["Medusa’s head seen in a well’s reflection","Violence turned into quiet romance","Final scene of the cycle"], terms:[]}
  ]},

 /* ================= WEEK 5 · PART 2 ================= */
 {n:12, wk:5, title:"Heracles", sub:"The greatest hero and his twelve labors",
  overview:{img:"h12_pollaiuolo", cap:["Antonio del Pollaiuolo","Hercules and the Hydra","ca. 1475"],
   sum:"Heracles, “the glory of Hera,” was the strongest of the Greek heroes and the only one worshipped as a god across the Greek world. Son of Zeus and the mortal Alcmena, he was persecuted by Hera from birth, driven mad, and forced to perform twelve impossible labors. His life mixes superhuman deeds with violence, appetite, and suffering, and it ends with his rise to Olympus.",
   bul:["Son of Zeus and Alcmena; enemy of Hera","Twelve labors for King Eurystheus","Mortal hero who becomes a god"],
   terms:[["Alcmena and Amphitryon","Heracles’ mortal mother and her husband; Amphitryon fathered his twin, Iphicles."],["Eurystheus","King of Tiryns and Mycenae, Heracles’ cousin, who set the twelve labors."],["Labors, deeds, side-deeds","The labors (athloi) were the twelve tasks set by Eurystheus; deeds (praxeis) were other adventures; side-deeds (parerga) happened along the way."]]},
  slides:[
  {type:"work", key:"h12_farnese", artist:"After Lysippos", title:"Farnese Hercules", date:"Roman copy, early 3rd century CE, of a Greek bronze of the 4th century BCE", det:"Marble, signed by Glykon of Athens · National Archaeological Museum, Naples · photograph by Giorgio Sommer, 1860s",
   fn:"A colossal statue made for the Baths of Caracalla in Rome, where bathers would see the hero resting after his labors.",
   sum:"The hero leans exhausted on his club, draped with the skin of the Nemean lion. Behind his back he holds the golden apples of the Hesperides, so this is the end of his labors, not the beginning. Hera sent snakes to kill him in his cradle, but the infant strangled them; later she drove him mad, and he killed his first wife, Megara, and their children. To be cleansed of this blood guilt he served Eurystheus.",
   bul:["Resting hero with club and lion skin","Golden apples hidden behind his back","Labors as penance for killing his family"],
   terms:[["Megara","Heracles’ first wife, killed with their children when Hera drove him mad."],["Miasma","The blood pollution Heracles had to wash away through his labors."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h12_crossroads", artist:"Annibale Carracci", title:"The Choice of Hercules", date:"1596", det:"Oil on canvas · Museo di Capodimonte, Naples",
   fn:"The central ceiling painting of the private study (Camerino) of Cardinal Odoardo Farnese in his Roman palace.",
   sum:"In a story told by the Greek writer Prodicus, the young Heracles meets two women at a crossroads: Pleasure offers an easy life, while Virtue points to a steep, rocky path that leads to glory. Heracles chooses Virtue. Carracci set the scene above the desk of a young cardinal, as a reminder to choose duty over pleasure.",
   bul:["Pleasure vs. Virtue at a crossroads","Heracles chooses the hard road","Moral lesson for a young cardinal"],
   terms:[["Psychomachia","A “battle for the soul” between virtues and vices; Heracles at the crossroads became a favorite image of it."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h12_pollaiuolo", artist:"Antonio del Pollaiuolo", title:"Hercules and the Hydra", date:"ca. 1475", det:"Tempera on panel · Uffizi Gallery, Florence · one of a pair with Hercules and Antaeus",
   fn:"One of two small panels repeating large canvases of the labors made for the Medici palace in Florence, where Hercules was a civic hero.",
   sum:"The first labors cleared the land of monsters: the Nemean lion, whose skin Heracles wore, and the nine-headed Hydra of Lerna, which grew two heads for each one cut off until his nephew Iolaus burned the stumps. Then came the Golden Hind of Artemis, the Erymanthian Boar, the filthy stables of King Augeas, and the man-eating Stymphalian Birds. Pollaiuolo, an expert in anatomy, gives the hero taut, straining muscles against a wide Tuscan landscape.",
   bul:["Labors 1–6 in the Peloponnese","Hydra’s heads seared by Iolaus","Pollaiuolo’s study of muscles in action"],
   terms:[["Labors 1–6","The Nemean Lion, the Lernaean Hydra, the Golden Hind of Artemis, the Erymanthian Boar, the Augean Stables, and the Stymphalian Birds."],["Iolaus","Heracles’ nephew and loyal companion, who helped him defeat the Hydra."],["Chiron","Wise centaur accidentally wounded by Heracles during the boar hunt; he gave up his immortality."],["Olympic Games","Said to have been founded by Heracles after he cleaned the Augean stables."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h12_antaios", artist:"Euphronios", title:"Krater with Heracles wrestling Antaeus", date:"Athenian, ca. 515–510 BCE", det:"Red-figure calyx krater · Louvre, Paris",
   fn:"A large bowl for mixing wine and water at a symposium, by one of the first great red-figure painters.",
   sum:"On his way to the apples of the Hesperides, Heracles fought Antaeus, a giant son of Earth who grew stronger every time he touched the ground. Heracles lifted him into the air and crushed him. Euphronios contrasts the neat, controlled hero with the wild-haired giant whose eyes roll in pain, a picture of civilization defeating savagery.",
   bul:["Antaeus draws strength from Earth","Heracles lifts and crushes him","Early red-figure anatomy"],
   terms:[["Antaeus","Libyan giant, son of Gaea, invincible while touching the earth."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h12_olympia", artist:"", title:"Metope with Heracles, Atlas, and Athena", date:"ca. 470–456 BCE", det:"Marble · Archaeological Museum of Olympia",
   fn:"One of twelve metopes of the labors on the Temple of Zeus at Olympia, the sanctuary Heracles was said to have founded.",
   sum:"The later labors took Heracles to the edges of the world: the Cretan Bull, the man-eating Mares of Diomedes, the belt of the Amazon queen Hippolyta, the cattle of the monster Geryon, and the golden apples of the Hesperides. For the apples, Heracles held up the sky while Atlas fetched them. Here Atlas returns with the apples while Athena quietly helps Heracles bear the weight.",
   bul:["Labors 7–11 at the ends of the earth","Heracles holds the sky for Atlas","Athena helps him from behind"],
   terms:[["Labors 7–11","The Cretan Bull, the Mares of Diomedes, the Belt of the Amazon Hippolyta, the Cattle of Geryon, and the Apples of the Hesperides."],["Pillars of Heracles","The rocks at the Strait of Gibraltar, said to have been set up by Heracles on his way to Geryon."],["Alcestis","Queen whom Heracles rescued by wrestling Death, a side-deed on his way to the Mares of Diomedes."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h12_andokides", artist:"Andokides Painter", title:"Amphora with Heracles and Cerberus", date:"Athenian, ca. 520–510 BCE", det:"Red-figure terracotta · Louvre, Paris",
   fn:"A storage jar decorated by one of the painters credited with inventing the red-figure technique.",
   sum:"The last and hardest labor was to bring Cerberus, the three-headed guard dog of the underworld, up to the living. Heracles crouches and reaches out to the dog almost gently, holding a chain, while Athena stands behind him. When Heracles brought Cerberus to Eurystheus, the frightened king hid in a storage jar. Going down to Hades and returning was the hero’s ultimate victory over death.",
   bul:["Labor 12: capture Cerberus","Heracles approaches the dog with a chain","A hero who returns from death"],
   terms:[["Red-figure","Greek vase technique (from ca. 530 BCE) leaving figures in the red clay against a black background, so details could be painted with a brush."]]},
  {type:"theme", title:"Labors that make the world safe", img:"h12_olympia", stmt:"Heracles clears the world of monsters so civilization can grow.",
   para:"Most of the labors pit the hero against wild beasts and savage beings at the edges of the known world. Each victory makes the land usable for farming, travel, and cities. Greeks across the Mediterranean claimed Heracles had visited them, and the Temple of Zeus at Olympia put all twelve labors on display as models of effort rewarded.",
   bul:["Monsters and savages defeated","Hero as founder and civilizer","Displayed at Olympia as a model"],
   terms:[], works:["h12_pollaiuolo","h12_antaios","h12_olympia","h12_andokides"]},
  {type:"work", key:"h12_nessus", artist:"Guido Reni", title:"Deianira Abducted by the Centaur Nessus", date:"1620–21", det:"Oil on canvas · Louvre, Paris",
   fn:"One of four scenes from Heracles’ life painted for Ferdinando Gonzaga, Duke of Mantua, for his villa.",
   sum:"Heracles’ second wife, Deianira, was carried off by the centaur Nessus, who offered to ferry her across a river; Heracles shot him with an arrow dipped in the Hydra’s poison. The dying centaur told Deianira to keep his blood as a love charm. Years later, jealous of a captive princess, she sent Heracles a robe soaked in it, and the poison burned his flesh. In agony he built his own funeral pyre on Mount Oeta.",
   bul:["Nessus seizes Deianira","Poisoned blood as a false love charm","The burning robe kills Heracles"],
   terms:[["Deianira","Heracles’ second wife, who unknowingly killed him with Nessus’s poisoned robe."],["Nessus","Centaur shot by Heracles whose blood became a deadly poison."],["Xenia","Guest-friendship; Heracles both honored and violated it in his travels."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h12_apotheosis", artist:"François Lemoyne", title:"The Apotheosis of Hercules", date:"1733–36", det:"Oil on canvas mounted on the ceiling · Salon d’Hercule, Palace of Versailles",
   fn:"A huge ceiling painting for a reception room at Versailles, flattering King Louis XV as a heroic, god-like ruler.",
   sum:"As Heracles’ body burned on the pyre, his mortal part was consumed and his divine part rose to Olympus. There he was reconciled with Hera and married her daughter Hebe, goddess of youth. Lemoyne fills the ceiling with more than a hundred figures welcoming the hero into heaven. Rulers loved the theme because it promised that great labors earn immortality.",
   bul:["Pyre on Mount Oeta","Rises to Olympus; marries Hebe","Royal symbol of earned glory"],
   terms:[["Apotheosis","The raising of a mortal to the status of a god."],["Hebe","Goddess of youth, daughter of Zeus and Hera, Heracles’ wife on Olympus."]]},
  {type:"theme", title:"The flawed hero", img:"h12_nessus", stmt:"Heracles’ strength is matched by his excesses and his suffering.",
   para:"Heracles kills his own family in madness, eats and drinks enormously, rages against gods and men, and dies through a wife’s mistaken jealousy. Greeks laughed at him in comedies and honored him in temples. Later Christians and moralists read his life as a struggle of virtue against appetite that ended in heaven, which is why rulers and churches alike used his image.",
   bul:["Madness, appetite, violence","Comic glutton and revered god","Read later as virtue rewarded"],
   terms:[], works:["h12_farnese","h12_crossroads","h12_nessus","h12_apotheosis"]}
  ]},

 /* ================= WEEK 6 · PART 1 ================= */
 {n:13, wk:6, title:"Theseus and Crete", sub:"Athens’ national hero, the Minotaur, and the Labyrinth",
  overview:{img:"h13_theseusvase", cap:["","Amphora with Theseus killing the Minotaur","mid-6th century BCE"],
   sum:"Athens claimed its people had sprung from its own soil, and its early kings were born from the earth. Its great hero was Theseus, who cleared the road to Athens of bandits, killed the Minotaur in Crete, and was remembered as the king who united Attica. His myths are tied to the island of Crete, where King Minos, the craftsman Daedalus, and the Labyrinth set the stage.",
   bul:["Athenians as “born from the earth”","Theseus: unifier and civic hero","Crete: Minos, Daedalus, the Minotaur"],
   terms:[["Autochthonous","“Sprung from the land itself,” the Athenian claim about their origins."],["Cecrops","Legendary first king of Athens, half man and half snake, who judged the contest between Athena and Poseidon."],["Erichthonius","Early Athenian king born from the earth after Hephaestus tried to assault Athena; raised secretly by Athena."]]},
  slides:[
  {type:"work", key:"h13_caryatid", artist:"Carl Friedrich Heinrich Werner", title:"The Porch of the Caryatids, Erechtheion", date:"1877", det:"Watercolor · Benaki Museum, Athens · view of the temple on the Athenian Acropolis (built 421–406 BCE)",
   fn:"A travel watercolor for collectors; the temple itself sheltered Athens’ oldest cults, including the tomb of Cecrops.",
   sum:"The Erechtheion on the Acropolis held the sacred spots of Athens’ origin myths: the mark of Poseidon’s trident, Athena’s olive tree, and the tomb of Cecrops. Athena gave the infant Erichthonius in a closed basket to Cecrops’ daughters and forbade them to look; two did, saw the snake-child, and leapt from the Acropolis in madness. Each year young girls called Arrhephoroi carried secret objects in baskets for Athena, recalling the story.",
   bul:["Shrine of Athens’ founding myths","Cecrops’ daughters open the forbidden basket","Ritual of the Arrhephoria"],
   terms:[["Daughters of Cecrops","Aglauros, Herse, and Pandrosos; those who opened Athena’s basket went mad."],["Arrhephoria","An Athenian ritual in which young girls carried secret objects for Athena by night."],["Caryatid","A column carved as a standing woman."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h13_procris", artist:"Piero di Cosimo", title:"A Satyr Mourning over a Nymph (The Death of Procris)", date:"ca. 1495", det:"Oil on panel · National Gallery, London",
   fn:"A long panel probably set into the wall paneling or furniture of a Florentine house.",
   sum:"The Athenian princess Procris married the hunter Cephalus, who was carried off for a time by Eos, the dawn goddess. Their marriage was poisoned by mutual testing and jealousy. Procris gave him a hound, Laelaps, that always caught its prey, and a spear that never missed; spying on him one day, she was killed by the spear when he mistook her for an animal. Piero shows a gentle satyr and the dog grieving over her body.",
   bul:["Cephalus, Procris, and Eos","Jealousy and a spear that never misses","Satyr and hound mourn her"],
   terms:[["Procris and Cephalus","Athenian couple whose jealousy ended when Cephalus killed Procris by accident."],["Laelaps","A hound that always caught its prey; in some versions Minos gave it to Procris."],["Eos (Aurora)","Goddess of the dawn, who carried off Cephalus."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h13_theseusvase", artist:"", title:"Amphora with Theseus killing the Minotaur", date:"Athenian, mid-6th century BCE", det:"Black-figure terracotta · Louvre, Paris",
   fn:"A storage jar painted with Athens’ favorite hero, popular as the city promoted Theseus in the 500s BCE.",
   sum:"Theseus was the son of King Aegeus of Athens (or of Poseidon) and Aethra of Troezen. Growing up, he lifted a boulder to find his father’s sword and sandals and walked to Athens, killing bandits on the way: the clubber Periphetes, Sinis the pine-bender, the sow of Crommyon, Sciron, the wrestler Cercyon, and Procrustes, who stretched or cut guests to fit his bed. At Athens Medea tried to poison him before Aegeus recognized the sword.",
   bul:["Son of Aegeus (or Poseidon) and Aethra","Six bandits on the road, then the Minotaur","Recognized by his father’s sword"],
   terms:[["Seven labors of Theseus","Periphetes, Sinis, the Crommyonian Sow, Sciron, Cercyon, Procrustes, and the Minotaur."],["Medea","Sorceress from Colchis, then wife of Aegeus, who tried to poison Theseus."],["Procrustes","Bandit who forced travelers to fit his bed by stretching or cutting them."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h13_pasiphae", artist:"", title:"Daedalus presenting the wooden cow to Pasiphaë", date:"Roman, 62–79 CE", det:"Wall painting · House of the Vettii, Pompeii",
   fn:"Part of the painted decoration of a reception room in a wealthy Pompeian house, paired with other myths of tragic love.",
   sum:"Minos, son of Zeus and Europa, became king of Crete. When he refused to sacrifice a magnificent bull to Poseidon, the god made his wife Pasiphaë desire it. The craftsman Daedalus built her a hollow wooden cow, and she bore the Minotaur, half man and half bull, which Minos shut inside the Labyrinth that Daedalus designed. Athens had to send seven youths and seven maidens to feed it.",
   bul:["Pasiphaë cursed with desire for a bull","Daedalus builds the wooden cow","Minotaur kept in the Labyrinth"],
   terms:[["Minos and Pasiphaë","King and queen of Crete; their children include Ariadne, Phaedra, and Androgeus."],["Daedalus","Athenian master craftsman who built the cow, the Labyrinth, and wings."],["Talos","A bronze giant who guarded Crete by circling it three times a day."],["Androgeus","Son of Minos killed in Attica; his death led Minos to demand the human tribute from Athens."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h13_canova", artist:"Antonio Canova", title:"Theseus and the Minotaur", date:"1782", det:"Marble · Victoria and Albert Museum, London",
   fn:"Canova’s first major work in Rome, carved from a block given by the Venetian ambassador Girolamo Zulian, who let him choose the subject; it became a manifesto of the new Neoclassical style.",
   sum:"Theseus volunteered to go to Crete with the tribute. Ariadne, Minos’s daughter, fell in love with him and gave him a ball of thread to find his way out of the Labyrinth. He killed the Minotaur and escaped with her. Canova shows the calm after the struggle: the hero sits quietly on the monster’s body, an image of restraint rather than violence.",
   bul:["Ariadne’s thread leads him out","Minotaur killed in the Labyrinth","Neoclassical calm after the fight"],
   terms:[["Labyrinth","The maze built by Daedalus to contain the Minotaur."],["Minotaur","Bull-headed son of Pasiphaë, killed by Theseus."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h13_ariadne", artist:"", title:"Sleeping Ariadne", date:"Roman copy, 2nd century CE, of a Hellenistic original", det:"Marble · Vatican Museums · photograph ca. 1920",
   fn:"A Roman garden or villa statue; in the Renaissance it was displayed as a fountain and long thought to show Cleopatra.",
   sum:"On the way home Theseus abandoned Ariadne asleep on the island of Naxos, where Dionysus found and married her. Theseus forgot to change his black sail to white, the sign of success, and his father Aegeus, seeing it, threw himself into the sea, now called the Aegean. Daedalus, imprisoned by Minos, escaped on wings with his son Icarus, and the Sicilian king Cocalus later protected him when Minos came hunting.",
   bul:["Ariadne left asleep on Naxos","Black sail: Aegeus’s death, the Aegean","Dionysus marries Ariadne"],
   terms:[["Ariadne","Minos’s daughter who helped Theseus, was abandoned on Naxos, and married Dionysus."],["Cocalus","King in Sicily who sheltered Daedalus; his daughters killed Minos."],["Scylla (of Megara)","A princess who betrayed her father Nisus for love of Minos and was turned into a bird."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h13_icarus", artist:"After Pieter Bruegel the Elder", title:"Landscape with the Fall of Icarus", date:"ca. 1560s", det:"Oil on canvas · Royal Museums of Fine Arts of Belgium, Brussels",
   fn:"A landscape for a collector, probably an early copy of a lost Bruegel painting.",
   sum:"Daedalus made wings of feathers and wax for himself and his son Icarus and warned him not to fly too high. Icarus flew toward the sun, the wax melted, and he fell into the sea. Bruegel shrinks the tragedy to a pair of legs splashing in the corner while a plowman, a shepherd, and a fisherman go on with their work, a reminder that the world takes little notice of one person’s fall.",
   bul:["Wings of feathers and wax","Icarus flies too high and falls","Tiny tragedy in a busy world"],
   terms:[["Icarus","Son of Daedalus who flew too near the sun and fell into the sea."]]},
  {type:"theme", title:"The hero of Athens", img:"h13_canova", stmt:"Athens built Theseus into its own Heracles.",
   para:"As Athens grew powerful in the 500s and 400s BCE, it promoted Theseus as a civic hero who cleared the roads, freed the city from Cretan tribute, and united Attica under one government. His cycle of labors was arranged to match Heracles’, and his image spread on vases and temples. Myth gave the young democracy a founder it could be proud of.",
   bul:["Labors designed to rival Heracles’","Freed Athens from Cretan tribute","Founder figure for the democracy"],
   terms:[], works:["h13_theseusvase","h13_canova","h13_amazon","h13_metope"]},
  {type:"work", key:"h13_amazon", artist:"", title:"Amazonomachy frieze from the Mausoleum at Halicarnassus", date:"ca. 350 BCE", det:"Marble · British Museum, London",
   fn:"Part of the sculptural decoration of the huge tomb of Mausolus, ruler of Caria, one of the Seven Wonders of the ancient world.",
   sum:"Theseus joined Heracles against the Amazons, warrior women from the edge of the world, and carried off their queen Antiope (sometimes called Hippolyta). The Amazons then invaded Athens and were defeated. Their son was Hippolytus. Greek artists loved the Amazonomachy as a battle of Greek men against a reversed world where women fight.",
   bul:["Theseus abducts the Amazon queen","Amazons invade and are beaten","Greeks vs. the “other”"],
   terms:[["Amazonomachy","The battle between Greeks and Amazons, a favorite subject of temple and tomb sculpture."],["Antiope (Hippolyta)","Amazon queen taken by Theseus; mother of Hippolytus."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h13_metope", artist:"", title:"Parthenon metope with a Lapith and a centaur", date:"447–438 BCE", det:"Marble, South Metope 31 · British Museum, London",
   fn:"One of the carved panels on the south side of the Parthenon, the temple of Athena on the Acropolis.",
   sum:"At the wedding of Theseus’s friend Pirithous, king of the Lapiths, and Hippodamia, the drunken centaurs tried to carry off the bride and the women, and a great fight followed. Theseus and Pirithous later tried to kidnap brides of their own, Helen of Sparta and even Persephone; Helen’s brothers, Castor and Pollux, rescued her, and the two heroes were trapped in the underworld until Heracles freed Theseus.",
   bul:["Centaurs attack the Lapith wedding","Theseus and Pirithous seek divine brides","Trapped in Hades; Heracles rescues Theseus"],
   terms:[["Centauromachy","The battle of the Lapiths and centaurs, an image of reason against animal passion."],["Pirithous and Hippodamia","King of the Lapiths and his bride, whose wedding the centaurs disrupted."],["Castor and Pollux","Twin brothers of Helen (the Dioscuri) who rescued her from Theseus."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h13_phaedra", artist:"Alexandre Cabanel", title:"Phèdre", date:"1880", det:"Oil on canvas · Musée Fabre, Montpellier",
   fn:"A Salon painting based on Racine’s tragedy Phèdre of 1677, itself drawn from Euripides.",
   sum:"Theseus later married Phaedra, Ariadne’s sister, who fell in love with her stepson Hippolytus. When he rejected her, she killed herself and left a letter accusing him. Theseus cursed his son, and Poseidon sent a bull from the sea that terrified Hippolytus’s horses and dragged him to his death. Cabanel shows Phaedra wasting away on her bed, consumed by forbidden love.",
   bul:["Phaedra loves her stepson Hippolytus","False accusation; Theseus’s curse","Euripides, then Racine"],
   terms:[["Phaedra","Daughter of Minos, wife of Theseus, who falsely accused her stepson."],["Hippolytus","Son of Theseus and the Amazon queen, devoted to Artemis, killed by his father’s curse."]]}
  ]},

 /* ================= WEEK 6 · PART 2 ================= */
 {n:14, wk:6, title:"Thebes, Oedipus, and Jason", sub:"A cursed city, a tragic king, and the voyage of the Argo",
  overview:{img:"h14_ingres", cap:["Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres","Oedipus and the Sphinx","1808"],
   sum:"Thebes was a city founded in blood and haunted by curses, and its royal family produced the most famous tragedy of all: Oedipus, who killed his father and married his mother without knowing it. Jason’s quest for the Golden Fleece, by contrast, is an adventure story, though it too ends in disaster when he betrays the sorceress Medea. Athenian playwrights turned both legends into tragedies still performed today.",
   bul:["Thebes: founded by Cadmus, cursed for generations","Oedipus: fate, guilt, and knowledge","Jason and Medea: quest and betrayal"],
   terms:[["Sophocles, Oedipus the King","Tragedy (ca. 429 BCE) in which Oedipus discovers his own crimes; Aristotle’s model of tragic plot."],["Aeschylus, Seven against Thebes","Tragedy (467 BCE) about the war between Oedipus’s sons."],["Sophocles, Antigone","Tragedy in which Antigone buries her brother against the king’s order."],["Apollonius of Rhodes, Argonautica","Hellenistic epic (3rd century BCE) of Jason’s voyage."],["Euripides, Medea","Tragedy (431 BCE) in which Medea takes revenge on Jason."]]},
  slides:[
  {type:"work", key:"h14_cadmus", artist:"Cornelis van Haarlem", title:"Two Followers of Cadmus Devoured by a Dragon", date:"1588", det:"Oil on canvas · National Gallery, London",
   fn:"A dramatic showpiece of Dutch Mannerism; Hendrick Goltzius made a famous engraving after it the same year, which spread the design.",
   sum:"Cadmus, searching for his sister Europa, was told by Delphi to found a city where a cow lay down. When his men went for water, a dragon sacred to Ares killed them; Cadmus slew it and, on Athena’s advice, sowed its teeth, from which armed men sprang up and fought until five remained, the Spartoi, ancestors of Thebes’ nobles. Van Haarlem shows the dragon tearing into the companions in the foreground while Cadmus fights in the distance.",
   bul:["Cadmus follows a cow to found Thebes","Dragon of Ares kills his men","Sown teeth become the Spartoi"],
   terms:[["Cadmus","Phoenician prince who founded Thebes; he married Harmonia, daughter of Ares and Aphrodite."],["Spartoi","“Sown men,” warriors grown from the dragon’s teeth."],["Harmonia","Daughter of Ares and Aphrodite, wife of Cadmus; her cursed necklace brought ruin to their descendants."],["Amphion and Zethus","Twin sons of Zeus and Antiope who built Thebes’ walls; Amphion married Niobe."],["Dirce","Queen who mistreated Antiope and was tied to a bull by the twins."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h14_kylix", artist:"Oedipus Painter", title:"Cup with Oedipus and the Sphinx", date:"Athenian, ca. 470 BCE", det:"Red-figure kylix · Vatican Museums",
   fn:"A drinking cup for the symposium, with the picture inside the bowl.",
   sum:"An oracle warned King Laius of Thebes that his son would kill him, so he and Queen Jocasta exposed their baby with his ankles pinned, giving him the name Oedipus, “swollen foot.” A shepherd saved him, and he was raised by King Polybus and Queen Merope of Corinth. Fleeing a prophecy that he would kill his father, he killed a stranger at a crossroads: Laius. At Thebes he answered the Sphinx’s riddle, which this cup shows him pondering.",
   bul:["Exposed as a baby, raised in Corinth","Kills Laius at a crossroads","Ponders the Sphinx’s riddle"],
   terms:[["Laius and Jocasta","King and queen of Thebes, Oedipus’s parents."],["Polybus and Merope","King and queen of Corinth who raised Oedipus as their son."],["Sphinx","A winged lion with a woman’s head who killed all who failed her riddle."],["Riddle of the Sphinx","What walks on four legs in the morning, two at noon, and three in the evening? A human: crawling, walking, then with a cane."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h14_ingres", artist:"Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres", title:"Oedipus and the Sphinx", date:"1808 (enlarged 1827)", det:"Oil on canvas · Louvre, Paris",
   fn:"A required study sent from Rome to the French Academy, later enlarged and sold; it made the nude hero a Neoclassical ideal.",
   sum:"Oedipus solved the riddle, the Sphinx killed herself, and grateful Thebes made him king and gave him the widowed queen Jocasta. They had four children: Antigone, Ismene, Polynices, and Eteocles. When plague struck, Oedipus vowed to find Laius’s killer and slowly discovered that it was himself, and that his wife was his mother. Jocasta hanged herself and Oedipus blinded himself. Ingres shows the calm, confident hero before he knows the truth.",
   bul:["Answers the riddle, becomes king","Discovers he killed his father, wed his mother","Blinds himself; Jocasta dies"],
   terms:[["Anagnorisis","Recognition: the moment a tragic character discovers the truth."],["Peripeteia","Reversal: the sudden turn from good fortune to bad."],["Antigone, Ismene, Polynices, Eteocles","The children of Oedipus and Jocasta."]]},
  {type:"theme", title:"Tragedy: knowledge that destroys", img:"h14_kylix", stmt:"Oedipus’s search for truth is what ruins him.",
   para:"Aristotle praised Oedipus the King because its recognition and reversal happen at the same moment: the more Oedipus learns, the further he falls. The play raises questions with no easy answer. Was Oedipus guilty if he acted in ignorance? Can anyone escape a prophecy? Tragedy let Athenian audiences feel pity and fear safely, and these legends of Thebes became its richest source.",
   bul:["Recognition and reversal together","Guilt without intent; fate vs. choice","Pity and fear, felt safely in the theater"],
   terms:[], works:["h14_kylix","h14_ingres","h14_antigone"]},
  {type:"work", key:"h14_antigone", artist:"Jules-Eugène Lenepveu", title:"Antigone Gives Token Burial to the Body of Her Brother Polynices", date:"1835–98", det:"Watercolor, pen and ink over black chalk · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York",
   fn:"A drawing by a French academic history painter; the museum does not record what it was made for.",
   sum:"After Oedipus left Thebes, his sons agreed to share the throne, but Eteocles refused to give it up. Polynices raised an army, the Seven against Thebes, and the brothers killed each other. Their uncle Creon, now king, forbade burial of the traitor Polynices. Their sister Antigone buried him anyway, choosing the laws of the gods over the king’s command, and was sentenced to be walled up alive.",
   bul:["Brothers kill each other: the Seven against Thebes","Creon forbids burial","Antigone obeys the gods, not the king"],
   terms:[["Seven against Thebes","The seven champions who attacked Thebes on behalf of Polynices."],["Creon","Jocasta’s brother, king of Thebes after the war, who condemned Antigone."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h14_jason", artist:"Bertel Thorvaldsen", title:"Jason with the Golden Fleece", date:"1802–03 (marble 1828)", det:"Marble · Thorvaldsens Museum, Copenhagen",
   fn:"The model that made the Danish sculptor famous in Rome; the marble was commissioned by the English banker Thomas Hope.",
   sum:"The Golden Fleece came from a flying ram sent to rescue Phrixus and Helle, children of King Athamas, from their stepmother Ino; Helle fell into the sea (the Hellespont), and Phrixus reached Colchis, where King Aeetes hung the fleece in a grove guarded by a dragon. Jason, whose uncle Pelias had taken the throne of Iolcus, was sent to fetch it. Thorvaldsen shows him striding home with the fleece over his arm, modeled on ancient statues.",
   bul:["Ram rescues Phrixus and Helle","Pelias sends Jason for the fleece","Neoclassical hero modeled on ancient statues"],
   terms:[["Phrixus and Helle","Children of Athamas and Nephele saved by the golden ram; Helle fell into the Hellespont."],["Aeetes","King of Colchis, son of Helios, keeper of the fleece and father of Medea."],["Pelias","Jason’s uncle, who seized the throne of Iolcus."],["Argo and Argonauts","The ship built by Argus, and the heroes who sailed in her, including Heracles, Orpheus, Castor, and Pollux."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h14_medea", artist:"Eugène Delacroix", title:"Medea about to Kill Her Children", date:"1838", det:"Oil on canvas · Palais des Beaux-Arts, Lille",
   fn:"A Salon painting bought by the French state, a showpiece of Romantic drama.",
   sum:"On the voyage the Argonauts met the women of Lemnos, King Cyzicus, the boxer Amycus, and the blind prophet Phineus, whom the winged brothers Zetes and Calais freed from the Harpies; Heracles was left behind searching for his beloved Hylas. In Colchis, Aeetes’ daughter Medea used her magic to help Jason yoke fire-breathing bulls and take the fleece, and on the way home she brought down the bronze giant Talos. Years later Jason abandoned her for a Corinthian princess, Glauce, and Medea killed the bride and her own children. Delacroix shows her hiding in a cave, dagger in hand, as her pursuers approach.",
   bul:["Adventures of the Argo","Medea’s magic wins the fleece","Abandoned, she kills her children"],
   terms:[["Medea","Colchian princess and sorceress who helped Jason and took revenge when he left her."],["Phineus and the Harpies","Blind prophet tormented by Harpies who snatched his food until the Argonauts drove them off."],["Hylas","Heracles’ young companion, pulled into a spring by nymphs."],["Talos and ichor","Bronze giant of Crete whose single vein of ichor, the gods’ blood, Medea drained."],["Glauce","Corinthian princess Jason planned to marry, killed by Medea’s poisoned robe."]]},
  {type:"theme", title:"The foreign woman", img:"h14_jason", stmt:"Medea is both the hero’s helper and his ruin.",
   para:"Like Ariadne for Theseus, Medea betrays her own family to help a Greek hero, and like Ariadne she is abandoned. But Medea fights back. Euripides makes her the most frightening woman on the Greek stage, a foreigner with magic powers who refuses to be discarded. Audiences were invited both to fear her and to feel the injustice done to her.",
   bul:["Helper who betrays her family","Abandoned by the hero","Feared and pitied: Euripides’ Medea"],
   terms:[], works:["h14_jason","h14_medea","h13_ariadne"]},
  {type:"work", key:"h14_meleager", artist:"", title:"Sarcophagus with the Calydonian Boar Hunt", date:"Roman, 2nd–3rd century CE", det:"Marble · Capitoline Museums, Rome",
   fn:"A coffin for a wealthy Roman; hunting myths celebrated the courage of the dead.",
   sum:"Artemis sent a monstrous boar to ravage Calydon, and Prince Meleager gathered heroes, among them the huntress Atalanta, to kill it. Meleager gave her the prize because she drew first blood, and in the quarrel that followed he killed his uncles. His mother, Althaea, then burned the log on which his life depended, and he died. The sarcophagus packs hunters, dogs, and the boar into one crowded frieze.",
   bul:["Artemis’s boar ravages Calydon","Atalanta draws first blood","Meleager’s life tied to a burning log"],
   terms:[["Meleager","Calydonian prince who led the boar hunt and died when his mother burned his life-log."],["Calydonian Boar Hunt","The great hunt that gathered heroes from all over Greece."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h14_atalanta", artist:"Guido Reni", title:"Atalanta and Hippomenes", date:"ca. 1620–25", det:"Oil on canvas · Museo di Capodimonte, Naples",
   fn:"A large mythological painting; Reni painted two versions, the other now in the Prado, Madrid.",
   sum:"Atalanta, a swift huntress who wished to stay unmarried, raced every suitor and killed those she beat. Hippomenes (or Melanion) prayed to Aphrodite, who gave him three golden apples, from the Garden of the Hesperides or, in Ovid, from her own sacred grove on Cyprus. During the race he dropped them one by one, and Atalanta stopped to pick them up and lost. Reni turns the race into a slow, elegant dance of two pale bodies.",
   bul:["Atalanta races her suitors","Aphrodite’s golden apples","She stops to pick them up and loses"],
   terms:[["Atalanta","Virgin huntress who joined the boar hunt and was won in a footrace."],["Hippomenes","Suitor who beat Atalanta with Aphrodite’s golden apples."]]}
  ]},

 /* ================= WEEK 7 · PART 1 ================= */
 {n:15, wk:7, title:"The Trojan War", sub:"The House of Atreus, Achilles, and the fall of Troy",
  overview:{img:"h15_paris", cap:["Peter Paul Rubens","The Judgement of Paris","ca. 1632–35"],
   sum:"The Trojan War was the defining legend of the Greek heroic age. It began with a golden apple and a beauty contest, drew every great Greek hero to the walls of Troy for ten years, and ended with the city’s destruction and the long, deadly homecomings of the victors. Homer’s Iliad tells only a few weeks of the tenth year, centered on the anger of Achilles.",
   bul:["Cause: the Judgment of Paris and Helen","Ten-year siege; Iliad covers weeks of year ten","Fall of Troy and the troubled homecomings"],
   terms:[["Homer, Iliad","Epic poem of the quarrel between Achilles and Agamemnon and its deadly results."],["House of Atreus","The cursed family of Tantalus, Pelops, Atreus, Agamemnon, and Orestes."],["Tantalus and Pelops","Tantalus served his son Pelops to the gods; restored to life, Pelops gave his name to the Peloponnese."],["Oenomaus, Hippodamia, Myrtilos","Pelops won Hippodamia by bribing the charioteer Myrtilos to sabotage her father’s chariot, then killed him, bringing a curse on his line."],["Atreus and Thyestes","Brothers whose feud led Atreus to serve Thyestes his own sons; Thyestes’ son Aegisthus later took revenge."]]},
  quote:{q:"Sing, O goddess, the anger of Achilles son of Peleus, that brought countless ills upon the Achaeans.", by:"Homer, Iliad 1.1–2, trans. Samuel Butler (1898)"},
  slides:[
  {type:"work", key:"h15_francois", artist:"Kleitias (painter) and Ergotimos (potter)", title:"The François Vase", date:"ca. 570–565 BCE", det:"Black-figure volute krater · National Archaeological Museum, Florence · shown in an early-20th-century drawing",
   fn:"A great wine-mixing bowl found in an Etruscan tomb at Chiusi; its bands of about 270 figures, many labeled with their names, make it a picture book of myth.",
   sum:"The main band shows the gods arriving at the wedding of the mortal Peleus and the sea goddess Thetis, whose son would be Achilles. At that wedding Eris, goddess of strife, who had not been invited, threw in a golden apple marked “for the fairest.” Other bands show Achilles’ later deeds and Ajax carrying his body. Because so many figures are labeled, the vase is a key document of how Greeks told these stories.",
   bul:["Wedding of Peleus and Thetis","About 270 figures, many labeled","A pictorial encyclopedia of myth"],
   terms:[["Peleus and Thetis","Mortal hero and sea goddess, parents of Achilles."],["Eris","Goddess of strife who threw the golden apple."],["Tyndareus and Leda","King and queen of Sparta; by Zeus, Leda bore Helen and Pollux, by Tyndareus, Clytemnestra and Castor."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h15_paris", artist:"Peter Paul Rubens", title:"The Judgement of Paris", date:"ca. 1632–35", det:"Oil on panel · National Gallery, London",
   fn:"A large late painting by Rubens of a favorite subject for showing three ideal female nudes.",
   sum:"Zeus refused to decide which goddess deserved Eris’s apple and sent Hermes to ask the Trojan prince Paris, a son of King Priam and Hecuba, who was tending sheep on Mount Ida. Hera offered power, Athena victory in war, and Aphrodite the most beautiful woman in the world. Paris chose Aphrodite, and his prize was Helen, the wife of Menelaus of Sparta. Rubens shows Paris holding the apple as the three goddesses pose before him.",
   bul:["Hera, Athena, Aphrodite compete","Paris chooses Aphrodite and Helen","The cause of the Trojan War"],
   terms:[["Judgment of Paris","Paris’s choice of Aphrodite as fairest, which led to the abduction of Helen."],["Priam and Hecuba","King and queen of Troy, parents of Hector, Paris, Cassandra, and Polyxena."],["Oath of Tyndareus","Helen’s suitors swore to defend whoever married her, which bound them to fight at Troy."]]},
  {type:"theme", title:"A war that began with a choice", img:"h15_francois", stmt:"One moment of vanity and desire sets the whole heroic world at war.",
   para:"The golden apple, the beauty contest, and Helen’s departure are a chain of choices that commit gods and men to ten years of slaughter. The oath Helen’s suitors swore turns a private wrong into a war of all Greece. Later artists painted the Judgment of Paris more than almost any other myth, often as an excuse to show three nudes.",
   bul:["Eris’s apple, Paris’s choice","Suitors’ oath makes it a Greek war","Favorite subject of later painters"],
   terms:[], works:["h15_francois","h15_paris"]},
  {type:"work", key:"h15_iphigenia", artist:"", title:"The Sacrifice of Iphigenia", date:"Roman, 1st century CE", det:"Wall painting from the House of the Tragic Poet, Pompeii · National Archaeological Museum, Naples",
   fn:"Part of the decoration of a Pompeian house named for its many scenes of drama and poetry.",
   sum:"The Greek fleet gathered at Aulis, but Artemis held back the winds. The seer Calchas declared that Agamemnon, the commander, must sacrifice his daughter Iphigenia. In the painting she is carried to the altar while Calchas raises a knife and Agamemnon, at left, covers his face; above, Artemis appears with the deer that, in some versions, replaced the girl at the last moment. Clytemnestra never forgave her husband.",
   bul:["Fleet stalled at Aulis","Agamemnon sacrifices Iphigenia","A deer substituted in some versions"],
   terms:[["Iphigenia","Daughter of Agamemnon and Clytemnestra, sacrificed (or rescued by Artemis) at Aulis."],["Calchas","The Greek army’s seer."],["Palamedes","Clever Greek hero who exposed Odysseus’s feigned madness and was later framed by him."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h15_exekias", artist:"Exekias", title:"Amphora with Achilles and Ajax playing a game", date:"Athenian, ca. 540–530 BCE", det:"Black-figure terracotta · Vatican Museums",
   fn:"A storage jar signed by Exekias as both potter and painter, found in an Etruscan tomb at Vulci.",
   sum:"Achilles, raised by the wise centaur Chiron, was the greatest Greek warrior; his cousin Ajax was the next. Exekias imagines them in a quiet moment, leaning over a board game during a lull in the fighting, spears on their shoulders. Inscriptions give their throws, and Achilles, still wearing his helmet, is winning. The calm makes the violence everyone knows is coming more powerful.",
   bul:["Achilles and Ajax in a quiet moment","Board game during the siege","Exekias at the peak of black-figure"],
   terms:[["Achilles","Son of Peleus and Thetis, greatest Greek hero, invulnerable except at his heel in later versions."],["Ajax","Huge, steadfast Greek warrior, second only to Achilles."],["Chiron","Wise centaur who trained Achilles and other heroes."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h15_briseis", artist:"", title:"Briseis Led Away from Achilles", date:"Roman, 1st century CE", det:"Wall painting from the House of the Tragic Poet, Pompeii · National Archaeological Museum, Naples",
   fn:"Another painting from the same Pompeian house, probably copying a famous lost Greek painting.",
   sum:"The Iliad opens in the tenth year. Agamemnon had taken Chryseis, daughter of Apollo’s priest Chryses, and when Apollo sent a plague he had to return her. To make up his loss he took Achilles’ captive Briseis. Achilles, insulted, withdrew from the fighting and asked his mother Thetis to make the Greeks suffer. Here Patroclus leads Briseis away while Achilles sits in angry dignity.",
   bul:["Chryseis returned; Briseis taken","Achilles withdraws in anger","Patroclus hands Briseis over"],
   terms:[["Chryses and Chryseis","Priest of Apollo and his daughter, whose capture by Agamemnon brought a plague."],["Briseis","Achilles’ captive, taken by Agamemnon."],["Patroclus","Achilles’ closest companion."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h15_sarpedon", artist:"Euphronios", title:"Krater with the Death of Sarpedon", date:"Athenian, ca. 515 BCE", det:"Red-figure calyx krater · National Archaeological Museum of Cerveteri (formerly at the Metropolitan Museum)",
   fn:"A wine-mixing bowl looted from an Etruscan tomb, bought by the Metropolitan Museum in 1972 and returned to Italy in 2008.",
   sum:"With Achilles out of the fight, the Trojans drove the Greeks back to their ships. Patroclus put on Achilles’ armor and led a counterattack, killing Sarpedon, a son of Zeus who fought for Troy. Zeus allowed his son’s death but sent Sleep and Death to carry his body home. Euphronios shows the two winged brothers lifting the bleeding hero while Hermes watches.",
   bul:["Patroclus kills Sarpedon, son of Zeus","Sleep and Death carry him home","A famous case of looting and return"],
   terms:[["Sarpedon","Lycian prince, son of Zeus, ally of Troy, killed by Patroclus."],["Hypnos and Thanatos","Sleep and Death, twin brothers."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h15_andromache", artist:"Jacques-Louis David", title:"Andromache Mourning Hector", date:"1783", det:"Oil on canvas · École des Beaux-Arts, Paris, on deposit at the Louvre",
   fn:"The painting David submitted to win admission to the French Royal Academy, shown at the Salon of 1783.",
   sum:"Hector, Troy’s greatest defender, killed Patroclus. Grief-stricken, Achilles returned to battle in new armor made by Hephaestus, killed Hector, and dragged his body behind his chariot. Hector’s wife Andromache and their little son Astyanax lost everything. David shows Andromache beside Hector’s body, one hand reaching toward him and the other holding her child.",
   bul:["Hector kills Patroclus","Achilles kills and drags Hector","Andromache and Astyanax mourn"],
   terms:[["Hector","Eldest son of Priam, Troy’s chief defender."],["Andromache and Astyanax","Hector’s wife and infant son."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h15_priam", artist:"Alexander Ivanov", title:"Priam Begging Achilles for the Body of Hector", date:"1824", det:"Oil on canvas · Tretyakov Gallery, Moscow",
   fn:"A student painting at the St. Petersburg Academy of Arts, which awarded it a gold medal.",
   sum:"In the Iliad’s final book, old King Priam, guided by Hermes, crosses the Greek camp at night to beg Achilles for his son’s body. He kisses the hands that killed Hector and asks Achilles to remember his own father. The two enemies weep together, and Achilles returns the body. The poem ends with Hector’s funeral, not with the fall of Troy.",
   bul:["Priam goes to Achilles by night","Shared grief between enemies","Iliad ends with Hector’s funeral"], terms:[]},
  {type:"theme", title:"Glory and its cost", img:"h15_exekias", stmt:"Achilles chooses a short, glorious life over a long, forgotten one.",
   para:"Achilles knew he could live long in obscurity or die young with undying fame, and he chose fame. The Iliad honors that choice but never hides its price: the deaths of Patroclus, Sarpedon, and Hector, and the grief of fathers, wives, and children. Its last scene, Priam and Achilles weeping together, makes shared mortality the poem’s real subject.",
   bul:["Kleos (glory) vs. a long life","War’s cost to families","Shared grief ends the Iliad"],
   terms:[["Kleos","Glory or fame won by great deeds, the only immortality a Homeric hero could hope for."]], works:["h15_exekias","h15_briseis","h15_sarpedon","h15_priam"]},
  {type:"work", key:"h15_ajax1", artist:"", title:"Krater with the Suicide of Ajax", date:"Etruscan, ca. 400–350 BCE", det:"Red-figure calyx krater, said to be from Vulci · British Museum, London",
   fn:"A wine-mixing bowl made in Etruria, showing how Greek myths spread to Italy.",
   sum:"After Achilles was killed by an arrow from Paris, guided by Apollo, the Greeks awarded his armor to Odysseus instead of Ajax. Humiliated, Ajax went mad, then killed himself by falling on his sword. Earlier, Achilles had killed the Amazon queen Penthesilea and fallen in love with her as she died, and mocked by the soldier Thersites, killed him too. The painting shows Ajax kneeling over the sword fixed in the ground.",
   bul:["Death of Achilles","Armor given to Odysseus","Ajax falls on his sword"],
   terms:[["Penthesilea","Amazon queen and ally of Troy, killed by Achilles."],["Thersites","Common soldier who mocked the leaders and was struck down by Achilles."],["Armor of Achilles","The prize whose award to Odysseus drove Ajax to suicide."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h15_laocoon", artist:"Hagesandros, Athanodoros, and Polydoros of Rhodes", title:"Laocoön and His Sons", date:"ca. 1st century BCE–1st century CE", det:"Marble · Vatican Museums · photograph ca. 1860–1900",
   fn:"Found in Rome in 1506, probably from the palace of the emperor Titus; Pope Julius II bought it within weeks, and it inspired Michelangelo.",
   sum:"To take Troy the Greeks needed Achilles’ son Neoptolemus, the bow of Heracles, and the stolen statue of Athena called the Palladium. Then they built a wooden horse, hid soldiers inside, and pretended to sail away. The Trojan priest Laocoön warned against it and threw a spear at it; two giant serpents came from the sea and crushed him and his sons. In Virgil, Trojans took this as a sign and dragged the horse inside.",
   bul:["Neoptolemus, Heracles’ bow, the Palladium","Laocoön warns against the horse","Serpents crush him and his sons"],
   terms:[["Laocoön","Trojan priest killed by sea serpents after warning against the wooden horse."],["Neoptolemus","Achilles’ son, brought to Troy after his father’s death."],["Palladium","The ancient statue of Athena that protected Troy, stolen by Odysseus and Diomedes."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h15_horse", artist:"Giovanni Domenico Tiepolo", title:"The Procession of the Trojan Horse into Troy", date:"ca. 1760", det:"Oil on canvas · National Gallery, London",
   fn:"A small painting, probably a study for a larger work, by the son of Giambattista Tiepolo.",
   sum:"The Trojans pulled the horse into the city and celebrated. That night the Greeks climbed out, opened the gates, and Troy was sacked. Priam was killed at an altar, Astyanax thrown from the walls, Polyxena sacrificed at Achilles’ tomb, and Cassandra, Apollo’s disbelieved prophetess, dragged from Athena’s shrine. Euripides’ Trojan Women shows the captured queens waiting to be shipped away as slaves.",
   bul:["Horse hauled into the city","Night sack of Troy","Fate of the Trojan women"],
   terms:[["Trojan Horse","The hollow wooden horse that carried Greek soldiers into Troy."],["Cassandra and Polyxena","Daughters of Priam: Cassandra, cursed never to be believed, and Polyxena, sacrificed on Achilles’ tomb."],["Euripides, The Trojan Women","Tragedy (415 BCE) about the captive women after Troy’s fall."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h15_orestes", artist:"William-Adolphe Bouguereau", title:"Orestes Pursued by the Furies", date:"1862", det:"Oil on canvas · Chrysler Museum of Art, Norfolk, Virginia",
   fn:"A large Salon painting, the kind of dramatic classical scene that made academic painters’ reputations.",
   sum:"The returns home, or nostoi, were often deadly. Agamemnon came back to Mycenae with Cassandra and was murdered in his bath by Clytemnestra and her lover Aegisthus. Years later his son Orestes, urged by his sister Electra and Apollo, killed his mother and Aegisthus. The Furies, avengers of family blood, hounded him until Athena’s court in Athens acquitted him. Bouguereau shows Orestes covering his ears as the Furies point at his dead mother.",
   bul:["Agamemnon murdered on his return","Orestes avenges him by killing his mother","Furies pursue him; Athens acquits him"],
   terms:[["Nostoi","“Returns,” the stories of the Greek heroes’ voyages home after Troy."],["Aeschylus, Oresteia","Trilogy (458 BCE): Agamemnon, The Libation Bearers, and The Eumenides."],["Clytemnestra and Aegisthus","Agamemnon’s wife and her lover, who killed him."],["Orestes and Electra","Agamemnon’s children who avenged him; Orestes later married Hermione, daughter of Helen and Menelaus."]]}
  ]},

 /* ================= WEEK 7 · PART 2 ================= */
 {n:16, wk:7, title:"The Odyssey", sub:"Odysseus’s ten-year journey home",
  overview:{img:"h16_odland", cap:["","The Odyssey Landscapes: the Laestrygonians","ca. 60–40 BCE"],
   sum:"Homer’s Odyssey follows Odysseus, king of Ithaca, on his ten-year voyage home after Troy, and his wife Penelope and son Telemachus, who wait for him while suitors take over his palace. Where the Iliad celebrates strength, the Odyssey celebrates intelligence, endurance, and the right treatment of guests. Odysseus survives monsters, witches, and goddesses by his wits, then wins back his home in disguise.",
   bul:["Odysseus’s journey home from Troy","Cunning and endurance over strength","Guest-friendship tested at every stop"],
   terms:[["Homer, Odyssey","Epic poem of Odysseus’s return to Ithaca."],["Xenia","Guest-friendship: the sacred duty of host and guest, honored or broken in nearly every episode."]]},
  quote:{q:"Tell me, O Muse, of that ingenious hero who travelled far and wide after he had sacked the famous town of Troy.", by:"Homer, Odyssey 1.1–2, trans. Samuel Butler (1900)"},
  slides:[
  {type:"work", key:"h16_turner", artist:"J. M. W. Turner", title:"Ulysses Deriding Polyphemus", date:"1829", det:"Oil on canvas · National Gallery, London",
   fn:"A Royal Academy exhibition painting, one of Turner’s grandest treatments of classical myth.",
   sum:"After sacking the Cicones in Thrace and escaping the Lotus-Eaters, whose fruit made men forget home, Odysseus and his men were trapped in the cave of the Cyclops Polyphemus, who ate them two at a time. Odysseus got him drunk, told him his name was “Nobody,” blinded him with a stake, and escaped under the bellies of his sheep. As he sailed off he shouted his real name, and Polyphemus’s father Poseidon cursed his voyage. Turner shows the ship sailing into a blazing sunrise while the giant writhes on the cliff.",
   bul:["Cicones and Lotus-Eaters","“Nobody” blinds Polyphemus","Poseidon’s curse"],
   terms:[["Lotus-Eaters","People whose fruit made travelers forget their homes."],["Polyphemus","Cyclops son of Poseidon blinded by Odysseus."],["Curse of Poseidon","The god’s anger that kept Odysseus from home for ten years."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h16_odland", artist:"", title:"The Odyssey Landscapes: the Laestrygonians", date:"Roman, ca. 60–40 BCE", det:"Wall painting from a house on the Esquiline Hill, Rome · Vatican Library",
   fn:"Part of a continuous painted frieze of Odysseus’s adventures in a Roman house, found in 1848.",
   sum:"The wind-king Aeolus gave Odysseus a bag holding all the adverse winds, but within sight of Ithaca his men opened it, hoping for treasure, and were blown back. Next the cannibal Laestrygonians destroyed every ship but Odysseus’s own. The painter sets the tiny figures of giants hurling rocks in a vast, airy landscape, one of the earliest surviving landscape paintings in Western art.",
   bul:["Aeolus’s bag of winds opened","Laestrygonians sink the fleet","Early landscape painting"],
   terms:[["Aeolus","King of the winds who tried to help Odysseus home."],["Laestrygonians","Cannibal giants who destroyed most of Odysseus’s ships."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h16_circe", artist:"John William Waterhouse", title:"Circe Offering the Cup to Ulysses", date:"1891", det:"Oil on canvas · Gallery Oldham, England",
   fn:"A Royal Academy painting; Waterhouse returned to enchantresses from myth throughout his career.",
   sum:"On the island of Aeaea the witch Circe turned Odysseus’s men into pigs. Hermes gave Odysseus a magic herb, moly, that protected him, and he forced Circe to restore his men; he then stayed with her for a year. Waterhouse puts the viewer in Odysseus’s place: Circe holds out the cup and wand, and Odysseus appears only as a reflection in the mirror behind her throne.",
   bul:["Circe turns men into pigs","Hermes’s herb, moly, protects Odysseus","Viewer stands in Odysseus’s place"],
   terms:[["Circe","Witch-goddess of Aeaea, daughter of Helios."],["Moly","Magic herb from Hermes that protected Odysseus from Circe’s drugs."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h16_sirensvase", artist:"Siren Painter", title:"Stamnos with Odysseus and the Sirens", date:"Athenian, ca. 480–470 BCE", det:"Red-figure terracotta · British Museum, London",
   fn:"A jar for mixing or storing wine, giving its name to the painter who decorated it.",
   sum:"After visiting the underworld, Odysseus sailed past the Sirens, whose song lured sailors to their deaths. He plugged his crew’s ears with wax and had himself tied to the mast so he could hear the song safely. The painter shows the Sirens as birds with women’s heads, one plunging toward the sea, perhaps in despair at her failure.",
   bul:["Sirens’ deadly song","Crew’s ears waxed; Odysseus tied to mast","Sirens as bird-women"],
   terms:[["Sirens","Bird-women whose irresistible song lured sailors onto the rocks."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h16_sirenswh", artist:"John William Waterhouse", title:"Ulysses and the Sirens", date:"1891", det:"Oil on canvas · National Gallery of Victoria, Melbourne",
   fn:"A Royal Academy painting that deliberately revived the ancient bird-woman form of the Sirens, rather than the mermaids of later art.",
   sum:"Waterhouse copies the Greek vase type, filling the sky with bird-bodied Sirens swooping around the ship. Beyond them lay the narrow strait between Scylla, a six-headed monster who snatched six of the crew, and Charybdis, a whirlpool. On the island of Thrinacia the starving crew ate the sacred cattle of Helios, and Zeus destroyed the ship, leaving Odysseus alone.",
   bul:["Bird-Sirens circle the ship","Scylla and Charybdis","Cattle of Helios eaten; crew destroyed"],
   terms:[["Scylla and Charybdis","A six-headed monster and a whirlpool on either side of a narrow strait; “between Scylla and Charybdis” means between two dangers."],["Cattle of Helios","Sacred cattle on Thrinacia; Eurylochus led the crew in eating them, and Zeus destroyed the ship."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h16_calypso", artist:"Arnold Böcklin", title:"Odysseus and Calypso", date:"1882–83", det:"Tempera on mahogany panel · Kunstmuseum Basel",
   fn:"A Symbolist painting for collectors, one of Böcklin’s moody mythological landscapes.",
   sum:"Odysseus was washed ashore on Ogygia, island of the nymph Calypso, who kept him for seven years and offered to make him immortal. He refused, longing for home and his mortal wife. Böcklin shows him as a dark, faceless figure staring out to sea from a rock while Calypso sits apart in red. The sea goddess Leucothea later saved him from a storm on his raft.",
   bul:["Seven years with Calypso","Refuses immortality for home","Saved at sea by Leucothea"],
   terms:[["Calypso","Nymph of Ogygia who kept Odysseus for seven years."],["Leucothea","The former Theban princess Ino, now a sea goddess, who gave Odysseus her veil to save him."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h16_nausicaa", artist:"Pieter Lastman", title:"Odysseus and Nausicaa", date:"1619", det:"Oil on panel · Alte Pinakothek, Munich",
   fn:"A history painting by Rembrandt’s teacher; its first owner is not recorded.",
   sum:"Shipwrecked and naked, Odysseus appeared before Princess Nausicaa and her maids as they washed clothes by the shore. Nausicaa kept calm and led him to her father’s palace, where the seafaring Phaeacians welcomed him, heard his story, and carried him home to Ithaca. Lastman shows the maids scattering in fright while the princess holds her ground.",
   bul:["Odysseus surprises Nausicaa","Phaeacians hear his tale","Their ship brings him home"],
   terms:[["Nausicaa","Phaeacian princess who helped Odysseus."],["Phaeacians","Hospitable sea people who carried Odysseus to Ithaca."]]},
  {type:"theme", title:"Wits over strength", img:"h16_turner", stmt:"Odysseus survives by cleverness, patience, and disguise.",
   para:"Odysseus is “much-turning,” a hero of tricks and lies as well as courage. He wins with false names, wax, ropes, and patience, and he loses men whenever they lack self-control. The poem also judges every host: monsters like Polyphemus eat their guests, while the good Phaeacians give gifts. The rules of guest-friendship are the moral backbone of the Odyssey.",
   bul:["Tricks: “Nobody,” the mast, disguises","Self-control saves, appetite destroys","Hosts judged by how they treat guests"],
   terms:[["Metis","Cunning intelligence, Odysseus’s great gift (also the name of Athena’s mother)."]], works:["h16_turner","h16_circe","h16_sirensvase","h16_nausicaa"]},
  {type:"work", key:"h16_penelope", artist:"Pinturicchio", title:"Penelope with the Suitors", date:"ca. 1509", det:"Fresco, detached · National Gallery, London",
   fn:"Painted for a room in the Siena palace of Pandolfo Petrucci, ruler of the city, celebrating faithful wives.",
   sum:"At home, more than a hundred suitors had moved into Odysseus’s palace, eating his wealth and pressing Penelope to remarry. She promised to choose once she finished weaving a shroud for her father-in-law Laertes, but unraveled the work every night. Odysseus, disguised as a beggar and helped by his loyal swineherd Eumaeus and his son Telemachus, strung his great bow, which no suitor could bend, and killed them all. Pinturicchio shows Penelope at her loom as the suitors and the disguised Odysseus enter.",
   bul:["Suitors consume Odysseus’s house","Penelope’s weaving trick","Odysseus returns in disguise and takes revenge"],
   terms:[["Penelope","Odysseus’s faithful and clever wife."],["Telemachus","Son of Odysseus and Penelope."],["Eumaeus","Odysseus’s loyal swineherd."],["Laertes","Odysseus’s aged father."],["Suitors of Penelope","Young nobles who occupied the palace and were killed by Odysseus."]]},
  {type:"theme", title:"The homecoming", img:"h16_calypso", stmt:"The Odyssey ends not in battle but in recognition.",
   para:"Odysseus must be recognized by his old dog, his nurse, his son, his wife, and finally his father before he is truly home. Penelope tests him with the secret of their bed, built around a living olive tree. Her loyalty and cleverness match his, and later writers treated her as the model of a faithful wife, in contrast to Clytemnestra.",
   bul:["Recognitions, one by one","Penelope tests him with the bed","Faithful Penelope vs. Clytemnestra"],
   terms:[], works:["h16_penelope","h16_calypso"]}
  ]},

 /* ================= WEEK 8 · PART 1 ================= */
 {n:17, wk:8, title:"Aeneas and the Founding of Rome", sub:"Virgil’s epic and Rome’s own legends",
  overview:{img:"h17_bernini", cap:["Gian Lorenzo Bernini","Aeneas, Anchises, and Ascanius","1618–19"],
   sum:"The Romans adopted the Greek gods but told their own foundation stories, and those stories were about duty, family, and the state. Virgil’s Aeneid, written for the emperor Augustus, makes the Trojan Aeneas the ancestor of Rome and of Augustus’s own family. Roman historians such as Livy then told of Romulus and Remus, the Sabine women, and the heroes of the early Republic.",
   bul:["Roman religion: spirits, duty, family","Aeneid: Troy to Italy, written for Augustus","Romulus, the Sabines, and the Republic"],
   terms:[["Numen (pl. numina)","Divine power or presence, often felt in places and objects."],["Sacrificium","A sacred offering, the center of Roman worship."],["Lares","Protective spirits of the household and crossroads."],["Penates","Gods of the family storeroom (penus), carried from Troy by Aeneas."],["Gens and familia","The clan sharing a family name, and the household under one father."],["Paterfamilias and patria potestas","The male head of the household and his legal power over its members."],["Genius and pietas","The life-spirit of a man or family, and the duty owed to gods, family, and country."],["Dis","A Roman name for the god of the underworld."]]},
  quote:{q:"Arms, and the man I sing, who, forc’d by fate, And haughty Juno’s unrelenting hate, Expell’d and exil’d, left the Trojan shore.", by:"Virgil, Aeneid 1.1–3, trans. John Dryden (1697)"},
  slides:[
  {type:"work", key:"h17_lararium", artist:"Luigi Bazzani", title:"The Lararium of the House of the Vettii, Pompeii", date:"1895", det:"Watercolor of a household shrine of ca. 62–79 CE",
   fn:"A record painted soon after excavation; the shrine itself was where the household offered daily worship to its protective spirits.",
   sum:"Roman religion centered on the home. In this shrine the household Lares dance on either side of the Genius, the spirit of the head of the family, while a serpent below protects the house. Families offered food, wine, and incense here daily. Romans renamed Greek gods for their own: Asclepius became Aesculapius, Persephone Proserpina, Odysseus Ulysses, and Hades Dis or Pluto.",
   bul:["Lares flank the family Genius","Daily offerings at home","Greek gods given Roman names"],
   terms:[["Lararium","A household shrine for the Lares."],["Aesculapius","The Roman name for Asclepius, god of medicine, brought to Rome in 291 BCE."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h17_herculesvictor", artist:"", title:"Temple of Hercules Victor", date:"Late 2nd century BCE", det:"Marble · Forum Boarium, Rome",
   fn:"A round temple in the cattle market beside the Tiber, near the Great Altar where Romans honored Hercules.",
   sum:"Romans told that Hercules, driving Geryon’s cattle back from Spain, stopped at the site of Rome, where the monster Cacus stole some of the herd. Hercules killed him, and King Evander set up the Ara Maxima, the Great Altar, in his honor. The cattle market, the Forum Boarium, grew up around it. This marble temple is one of the oldest surviving in Rome.",
   bul:["Hercules kills Cacus at Rome","Ara Maxima founded by Evander","Oldest surviving marble temple in Rome"],
   terms:[["Forum Boarium","Rome’s cattle market by the Tiber."],["Ara Maxima","The Great Altar of Hercules in the Forum Boarium."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h17_bernini", artist:"Gian Lorenzo Bernini (with Pietro Bernini)", title:"Aeneas, Anchises, and Ascanius", date:"1618–19", det:"Marble · Galleria Borghese, Rome",
   fn:"Commissioned by Cardinal Scipione Borghese for his villa; it was the young Bernini’s first life-size marble group.",
   sum:"Aeneas, son of Venus and the Trojan Anchises, escapes burning Troy carrying his old father, who holds the household gods, and leading his small son Ascanius, also called Iulus. Virgil wrote the Aeneid under Augustus, whose family, the Julii, claimed descent from Iulus. Bernini stacks the three generations into one spiral: the old man, the hero in his prime, and the child. The group is the image of Roman pietas.",
   bul:["Aeneas carries Anchises, leads Ascanius","Iulus: ancestor of the Julian family","Three generations = pietas"],
   terms:[["Aeneas","Trojan hero, son of Venus and Anchises, ancestor of the Romans."],["Ascanius (Iulus)","Aeneas’s son, claimed ancestor of Julius Caesar and Augustus."],["Augustus, Virgil, Livy, Ovid","Rome’s first emperor and the writers of his age who shaped Rome’s myths."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h17_guerin", artist:"Pierre-Narcisse Guérin", title:"Aeneas Telling Dido of the Misfortunes of Troy", date:"1815", det:"Oil on canvas · Louvre, Paris",
   fn:"A Neoclassical history painting, staged like a scene from a play, that was a great success at the Paris Salon of 1817.",
   sum:"Aeneas wandered for years: to Thrace, where a bleeding bush warned him away; past the Harpies; to Epirus, where the seer Helenus guided him; and to Sicily, where he saw the blinded Polyphemus. Storms drove him to Carthage, newly founded by Queen Dido. As he told her the story of Troy, she fell in love with him, encouraged by Venus and Cupid, shown here as Ascanius on her lap.",
   bul:["Wanderings: Thrace, Harpies, Epirus, Sicily","Arrival at Carthage","Dido falls in love as he tells his story"],
   terms:[["Dido","Phoenician queen and founder of Carthage."],["Helenus","Trojan prophet, son of Priam, ruling in Epirus."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h17_guercino", artist:"Guercino", title:"The Death of Dido", date:"1631", det:"Oil on canvas · Galleria Spada, Rome",
   fn:"Commissioned by Cardinal Bernardino Spada as a gift for Maria de’ Medici, queen of France; when that fell through he bought it himself in 1631, and it remains in his family’s palace collection.",
   sum:"Jupiter sent Mercury to remind Aeneas that his destiny lay in Italy, and he left Carthage in secret. Dido built a pyre, cursed the Trojans, and stabbed herself with his sword. Her curse explained the later Punic Wars between Rome and Carthage. Guercino shows her collapsed on the pyre as her sister and attendants rush to her, while Aeneas’s ships sail away in the distance.",
   bul:["Aeneas leaves at Jupiter’s command","Dido’s curse and suicide","Origin story of the Punic Wars"],
   terms:[["Punic Wars","Three wars (264–146 BCE) between Rome and Carthage, which Dido’s curse was said to foreshadow."]]},
  {type:"theme", title:"Duty before desire", img:"h17_primaporta", stmt:"Aeneas’s greatness lies in giving up what he wants for what he must do.",
   para:"Unlike Achilles or Odysseus, Aeneas does not fight for personal glory or for his own home. He carries his father and his gods, abandons Dido, and fights a war in Italy because fate and the future Rome require it. Virgil makes that pietas the Roman virtue, and Augustus presented himself as Aeneas’s heir. The poem also mourns the people, like Dido, crushed along the way.",
   bul:["Pietas: duty to gods, family, country","Personal desire sacrificed to destiny","Augustus as Aeneas’s heir"],
   terms:[], works:["h17_bernini","h17_guercino","h17_arapacis","h17_primaporta"]},
  {type:"work", key:"h17_arapacis", artist:"", title:"Ara Pacis Augustae: the sacrifice relief", date:"13–9 BCE", det:"Marble · Museum of the Ara Pacis, Rome · historical photograph",
   fn:"A panel of the Altar of Augustan Peace, voted by the Senate to celebrate Augustus’s return from Spain and Gaul.",
   sum:"In Italy Aeneas, guided by the Cumaean Sibyl, visited the underworld, passing the Mourning Fields of tragic lovers and reaching Elysium, where Anchises showed him the future heroes of Rome. Landing in Latium, he found the sign of a white sow, allied with King Evander, and won Lavinia, daughter of King Latinus, by defeating the Rutulian prince Turnus. This relief shows a veiled man sacrificing a sow, traditionally read as Aeneas, though some scholars argue it is Rome’s second king, Numa.",
   bul:["Underworld: Mourning Fields to Elysium","White sow, Evander, Latinus, Lavinia","Aeneas defeats Turnus"],
   terms:[["Latinus and Lavinia","King of Latium and his daughter, whom Aeneas married."],["Turnus","Rutulian prince and Aeneas’s rival, killed at the end of the Aeneid."],["Lavinium and Alba Longa","The town Aeneas founded and the city founded by Ascanius."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h17_primaporta", artist:"", title:"Augustus of Prima Porta", date:"Early 1st century CE, after a bronze of ca. 20 BCE", det:"Marble · Vatican Museums · photograph ca. 1875–1900",
   fn:"A statue found in 1863 at the villa of Augustus’s wife Livia, presenting the emperor as a commander and a near-divine leader.",
   sum:"Augustus stands like a Greek statue, addressing his troops, his breastplate showing the return of Roman standards from Parthia. At his feet a small Cupid rides a dolphin, a reminder that the Julian family descended from Venus through Aeneas. The statue puts Virgil’s myth into stone: the emperor as the destined heir of Troy.",
   bul:["Emperor as general and orator","Cupid on a dolphin: descent from Venus","Myth used as imperial propaganda"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h17_wolf", artist:"", title:"Capitoline Wolf", date:"Date debated (traditionally 5th century BCE; twins added ca. 1471–1509)", det:"Bronze · Capitoline Museums, Rome",
   fn:"The symbol of Rome; the twins were added in the Renaissance when the wolf was given to the people of Rome.",
   sum:"In Livy’s history, King Numitor of Alba Longa was overthrown by his brother Amulius, who forced Numitor’s daughter Rhea Silvia to become a Vestal Virgin. Mars fathered twins with her anyway, and Amulius had them thrown into the Tiber. A she-wolf nursed them until the shepherd Faustulus and his wife Larentia raised them; lupa, “wolf,” was also slang for a prostitute, which may explain the story. The wolf’s date is now disputed, with some tests suggesting it was cast in the Middle Ages.",
   bul:["Romulus and Remus, sons of Mars and Rhea Silvia","Nursed by a she-wolf","The wolf’s date is debated"],
   terms:[["Livy, Ab urbe condita","Livy’s history of Rome “from the founding of the city.”"],["Numitor and Amulius","Rival brothers, kings of Alba Longa; Numitor was the twins’ grandfather."],["Faustulus and Larentia","The shepherd and his wife who raised the twins."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h17_romulus", artist:"Peter Paul Rubens", title:"Romulus and Remus", date:"ca. 1615–16", det:"Oil on canvas · Capitoline Museums, Rome",
   fn:"Its first owner is not recorded; it is now in the museum on the Capitoline Hill, the heart of ancient Rome.",
   sum:"Rubens paints the she-wolf licking one twin while the river god Tiber reclines nearby and the shepherd Faustulus arrives. Grown up, the twins restored their grandfather and founded a new city, but in a quarrel Romulus killed Remus and named Rome for himself. After his death he was said to have been taken up to heaven and worshipped as the god Quirinus.",
   bul:["She-wolf, Tiber, and Faustulus","Romulus kills Remus, founds Rome","Romulus deified as Quirinus"],
   terms:[["Quirinus","The god Romulus became after his death."],["Apotheosis","Deification; Roman emperors were declared divus (divine) after death."],["Triumph","A victory parade through Rome in which the triumphant general dressed as Jupiter."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h17_sabine", artist:"Nicolas Poussin", title:"The Abduction of the Sabine Women", date:"ca. 1633–34", det:"Oil on canvas · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York",
   fn:"Owned in the 1630s by the French ambassador to Rome and later by Cardinal Richelieu; it is staged like an ancient relief among classical buildings.",
   sum:"Rome had too few women, so Romulus invited the neighboring Sabines to a festival and, at his signal, the Romans seized their daughters. Later the Sabine king Tatius attacked, helped by the traitor Tarpeia, who opened the citadel and was crushed under the Sabines’ shields. The Sabine women, led by Romulus’s wife Hersilia, ran between the armies and made peace, and the two peoples merged. Poussin shows Romulus giving the signal from the steps at left.",
   bul:["Romans seize the Sabine women","Tarpeia’s betrayal","The women make peace"],
   terms:[["Sabine women","Women seized by the first Romans who later reconciled Rome and the Sabines."],["Tarpeia","Roman girl who betrayed the citadel and was killed; traitors were thrown from the Tarpeian Rock."],["Hersilia and Tatius","Romulus’s Sabine wife, and the Sabine king who later shared rule with him."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h17_horatii", artist:"Jacques-Louis David", title:"The Oath of the Horatii", date:"1784–85", det:"Oil on canvas · Louvre, Paris",
   fn:"Commissioned by the French crown; its message of patriotic sacrifice was later read as a call to revolution.",
   sum:"In the war between Rome and Alba Longa, the two cities let three brothers from each side, the Roman Horatii and the Alban Curiatii, fight for them. David shows the Horatii swearing on swords held by their father, while the women, one engaged to a Curiatius, collapse in grief. Only one Horatius survived; when his sister mourned her dead fiancé, he killed her too.",
   bul:["Three brothers fight for Rome","Oath on their father’s swords","Patriotism above family"],
   terms:[["Horatii and Curiatii","Roman and Alban triplets whose combat decided the war."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h17_lucretia", artist:"Rembrandt", title:"Lucretia", date:"1664", det:"Oil on canvas · National Gallery of Art, Washington",
   fn:"A late painting focused on a single, tragic figure; its first owner is not recorded.",
   sum:"Sextus Tarquinius, son of Rome’s last king, raped the virtuous Lucretia, wife of Tarquinius Collatinus. She told her husband and father what had happened and stabbed herself. Lucius Junius Brutus swore on her blood to drive out the kings, and in 509 BCE the Romans founded the Republic. Rembrandt shows her in the moment before the blow, dagger raised, face full of sorrow.",
   bul:["Lucretia’s rape and suicide","Brutus’s oath","Kings expelled; Republic founded"],
   terms:[["Lucretia","Roman wife whose death led to the overthrow of the monarchy."],["Brutus and Collatinus","Leaders of the revolt and the first consuls of the Republic."]]},
  {type:"theme", title:"Myth as Roman history", img:"h17_horatii", stmt:"Rome’s legends taught citizens what Rome expected of them.",
   para:"Roman stories are set in Rome itself and told as history. They praise self-sacrifice for the state, punish betrayal, and often turn on the bodies of women, the Sabines, Tarpeia, Lucretia, whose suffering changes the course of politics. Later revolutions, from France to America, borrowed these legends as models of civic virtue.",
   bul:["Told as history, set in Rome","Duty and sacrifice for the state","Women’s fates drive political change"],
   terms:[], works:["h17_wolf","h17_sabine","h17_horatii","h17_lucretia"]}
  ]},

 /* ================= WEEK 8 · PART 2 ================= */
 {n:18, wk:8, title:"Interpreting Myth", sub:"How people have tried to explain what myths mean",
  overview:{img:"h18_primavera", cap:["Sandro Botticelli","Primavera","ca. 1480"],
   sum:"Almost as soon as myths were written down, people began asking what they really meant. Greek philosophers read them as hidden science or moral lessons, Christians as pagan echoes of the Bible, and modern scholars as ritual, language, psychology, social structure, or politics. Each theory reveals something, and each age’s favorite theory says as much about that age as about the myths.",
   bul:["Ancient: allegory and history","Christian and Renaissance: moral and spiritual meanings","Modern: ritual, language, mind, structure, context"],
   terms:[["Allegory","Reading a story as a coded message about something else."],["Symbolism","Treating figures and objects in a myth as signs for ideas."]]},
  slides:[
  {type:"text", title:"Greek theories", sub:"Myth as science, history, and morality",
   sum:"Greek thinkers were the first critics of myth. Some philosophers, offended by stories of gods behaving badly, read the myths as hidden truths about nature or ethics. Others treated them as distorted history.",
   bul:["Myths contain hidden philosophical or historical truth","Gods as forces of nature or virtues","Gods as deified ancient kings"],
   terms:[["Philosophical or historical truth","The view that myths preserve real knowledge or real events in disguised form."],["Physical allegory","Reading gods as natural forces: Zeus as sky, Hera as air, Hephaestus as fire."],["Etymology","Explaining a god’s nature from his or her name, a favorite method of Stoic writers."],["Euhemerism","The theory of Euhemerus (ca. 300 BCE) that gods were great human kings later worshipped."],["Moral allegory","Reading myths as lessons in virtue and vice, such as Heracles at the crossroads."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h18_ovide", artist:"", title:"Ovide moralisé en prose", date:"15th century", det:"Illuminated manuscript · Bibliothèque nationale de France, Paris",
   fn:"A French prose version of a moralized Ovid, made for an aristocratic reader who wanted the stories with their Christian meaning.",
   sum:"Medieval Christians kept reading Ovid by treating his myths as allegories of Christian truth. The long French poem called the Ovide moralisé (about 1300) retells the Metamorphoses and then explains each story: Phaethon’s fall stands for pride, Orpheus can stand for Christ. Prose versions like this one carried the method into the 1400s, and it shaped how Renaissance artists understood classical subjects.",
   bul:["Ovid retold with Christian morals","About 1300; prose versions later","Kept myth alive in the Middle Ages"],
   terms:[["Ovide moralisé","French medieval retelling of Ovid with Christian allegorical explanations."],["Alchemy","Medieval and Renaissance search for transforming metals, which read myths as coded recipes."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h18_primavera", artist:"Sandro Botticelli", title:"Primavera", date:"ca. 1480", det:"Tempera on panel · Uffizi Gallery, Florence",
   fn:"Made for the Medici family, probably for a room in the Florence palace of Lorenzo di Pierfrancesco de’ Medici, possibly for a wedding.",
   sum:"Venus stands in her garden with Cupid above, flanked by the Three Graces, Mercury, Flora, and the west wind Zephyr seizing the nymph Chloris. Many scholars read it through the Neoplatonism of the Florentine philosopher Marsilio Ficino, who taught that earthly beauty and love lead the soul upward toward the divine. Whatever its exact meaning, it shows how Renaissance thinkers treated myth as philosophy.",
   bul:["Venus, Graces, Mercury, Flora, Zephyr","Neoplatonic reading: love leads upward","Myth as philosophy in Medici Florence"],
   terms:[["Neoplatonism","A philosophy, revived in Renaissance Florence, that saw earthly beauty as a reflection of divine beauty."]]},
  {type:"text", title:"From the Enlightenment to anthropology", sub:"Myth as superstition, poetry, and ritual",
   sum:"Enlightenment thinkers saw myths as the mistakes of primitive minds, while Romantics prized them as the poetry of nations. In the 1800s and 1900s, scholars compared myths around the world and linked them to rituals and social customs.",
   bul:["Enlightenment: myths as early, mistaken science","Romantics: myths as national poetry and imagination","Anthropology: myths tied to ritual and society"],
   terms:[["Enlightenment theories","18th-century views of myth as superstition from humanity’s childhood."],["Romantic theories","19th-century views of myth as the creative expression of a people’s spirit."],["Ritual theory","The idea that myths grew out of rituals and explain them (James Frazer, Jane Harrison)."],["Charter theory","Bronisław Malinowski’s idea that myths justify a society’s customs and institutions."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h18_helios", artist:"", title:"Metope with Helios in his chariot", date:"Hellenistic, ca. 300–280 BCE", det:"Marble, from the Temple of Athena at Ilion (Troy) · Altes Museum, Berlin",
   fn:"A carved panel from the temple of Athena at Hellenistic Troy, found by Heinrich Schliemann.",
   sum:"The sun god rises in his four-horse chariot, his head crowned with rays. In the 1800s the philologist Max Müller argued that most myths began as poetic descriptions of the sun, dawn, and sky, misunderstood as language changed, so that a story like Daphne fleeing Apollo meant the dawn fleeing the sun. Solar mythology faded, but comparing myths across Indo-European languages remained influential, notably in Georges Dumézil’s theory of three social functions.",
   bul:["Max Müller: myths as misunderstood sun poetry","Comparing Indo-European myths","Dumézil: priests, warriors, producers"],
   terms:[["Solar mythology","Max Müller’s theory that myths began as descriptions of the sun and sky."],["Indo-European comparative mythology","Comparing myths of peoples with related languages to reconstruct a shared ancestor."],["Linguistic theories","Explanations of myth from the history of words and language."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h18_moreau", artist:"Gustave Moreau", title:"Oedipus and the Sphinx", date:"1864", det:"Oil on canvas · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York",
   fn:"The painting that made Moreau famous at the Paris Salon of 1864, an early landmark of Symbolism.",
   sum:"Moreau’s Sphinx clings to Oedipus’s chest, a beautiful and deadly woman locked in a staring match with the hero, above the remains of earlier victims. Within a few decades Sigmund Freud would read the Oedipus myth as a key to the mind, and Carl Jung would see myths as expressions of a shared unconscious. Psychology gave myth a new meaning: stories about the hidden self.",
   bul:["Symbolist femme fatale and hero","Freud: the Oedipus complex","Jung: archetypes and the collective unconscious"],
   terms:[["Psychological allegory","Reading myth as a picture of inner, mental conflict."],["Condensation and displacement","Freud’s terms for how dreams, and myths, compress and shift meaning."],["Oedipus complex","Freud’s theory of a child’s desire for one parent and rivalry with the other."],["Electra complex","A term (from Jung) for the female version of the Oedipus complex."],["Collective unconscious and archetypes","Jung’s idea of a shared layer of the mind containing universal patterns, such as the hero or the great mother."]]},
  {type:"text", title:"Structure and context", sub:"Myth as a system of signs, and as a product of its time",
   sum:"In the 20th century, structuralists looked for the hidden logic that organizes myths, while other scholars insisted on reading each myth in its historical and social setting, including how it treats women.",
   bul:["Structuralism: myths as systems of opposites","Paris School: myth within Greek society and ritual","Contextual and feminist readings"],
   terms:[["Structuralism","Claude Lévi-Strauss’s method of reading myths as patterns of opposites (nature/culture, raw/cooked) that a culture tries to resolve."],["Semiotics: signifier and signified","The study of signs: the signifier is the word or image, the signified the idea it stands for."],["Paris School of myth criticism","Jean-Pierre Vernant, Marcel Detienne, and Pierre Vidal-Naquet, who read Greek myth through Greek society, politics, and sacrifice."],["Programs of action","The idea that myths model how people should act in their society."],["Feminist criticism","Reading myths for how they define and control women, and recovering women’s perspectives."]]},
  {type:"theme", title:"Why myths last", img:"h18_moreau", stmt:"Myths survive because every age can find itself in them.",
   para:"No single theory explains all myths. A story like Oedipus can be ritual, history, psychology, and politics at once, and artists from Athenian vase painters to Moreau have each found something new in it. That openness is why these ancient stories keep returning in art, literature, and film.",
   bul:["No single key to all myths","Each age reads itself into them","Endless reinterpretation in art"],
   terms:[], works:["h18_ovide","h18_primavera","h18_helios","h18_moreau"]}
  ]}
);
